--------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nFix a row \\(i\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (the uncovered square is the leftmost one) then the remaining squares of row \\(i\\) form the contiguous interval \\(\\{(i,c)\\mid 2\\le c\\le 2025\\}\\).  \nA single tile can cover this whole interval, so row \\(i\\) needs **at least one** tile.  \n\nIf \\(\\pi(i)=2025\\) (the uncovered square is the rightmost one) the row’s remaining squares form the interval \\(\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le c\\le 2024\\}\\); again one tile suffices.  \n\nIf \\(2\\le \\pi(i)\\le 2024\\) the row is split into two non‑empty intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le c<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,c)\\mid \\pi(i)<c\\le 2025\\}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain squares from both \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\), because any rectangle that spans the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) would also contain the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence row \\(i\\) requires **at least two** distinct tiles, one covering \\(L_i\\) and one covering \\(R_i\\).\n\nSince each column contains exactly one uncovered square, the column \\(1\\) can be the location of an uncovered square for **at most one** row, and similarly column \\(2025\\) can be the location of an uncovered square for at most one row.  \nTherefore at most two rows can be of the “edge” type (uncovered square in column 1 or 2025); all remaining \\(2025-2\\) rows are of the interior type and need at least two tiles each.\n\nConsequently the total number \\(T\\) of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nT \\ge (2025-2)\\cdot 2 + 2\\cdot 1 = 4048 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with 4048 tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Left‑of‑diagonal tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le 2024\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_c =