ecisely the diagonal cells\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i):1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nAll other cells, i.e. the set\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j):i\\neq j\\},\n\\]\nmust be covered by axis‑aligned rectangular tiles whose interiors are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) define  \n\n* the **left segment** \\(L_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<i\\}\\) (empty when \\(i=1\\)),  \n* the **right segment** \\(R_i=\\{(i,j):i<j\\le n\\}\\) (empty when \\(i=n\\)).\n\nBoth \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are contiguous in the row, hence each is a rectangle.  \nThe family\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}=\\{L_i : 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{R_i : 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThese rectangles are pairwise disjoint and their union is precisely \\(U\\); they avoid all diagonal cells.  \nThus a covering with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nConsider the set of off‑diagonal cells\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,j):2\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(i,1):2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nClearly \\(|S|=2n-2\\) and every element of \\(S\\) belongs to \\(U\\), so each must be covered by some tile.\n\nLet a tile be a rectangle \\(R=A\\times B\\) where \\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of rows and \\(B\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set of columns it occupies.  \nIf \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\), say \\((1,j)\\) with \\(j>1\\) and \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i>1\\), then \\(1\\in A\\) and \\(i\\in A\\) and also \\(1\\in B\\) and \\(j\\in B\\).  \nSince a rectangle contains the Cartesian product of its row and column sets, it would also contain the cell \\((1,1)\\).  \nBut \\((1,1)\\) is a diagonal cell and must remain uncovered; hence such a rectangle cannot exist.\n\nTherefore **each rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\)**.  \nTo cover 