### 2.  Decomposition of the covered part  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the squares left of the hole are  \n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\]\nand the squares right of the hole are  \n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]  \nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]  \nThe set of all covered squares is \\(L\\cup R\\); the two sets are disjoint.\n\nA tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle, i.e. a set of the form  \n\\[\n[a,b]\\times[c,d]=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\},\n\\]  \nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nA tile may lie entirely in \\(L\\) (a *left\u2011type* tile) or entirely in \\(R\\) (a *right\u2011type* tile); it cannot intersect both because any rectangle containing a hole would be illegal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left side  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the **left\u2011adjacent cell**\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]  \nThe cells \\(\\ell_i\\) are precisely the rightmost cells of the left part of each row.  \nBecause the values \\(p(i)-1\\) are all distinct, the \\(\\ell_i\\) occupy distinct columns\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\**Lemma 1.** *A left\u2011type rectangle can contain at most one left\u2011adjacent cell.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a left\u2011type rectangle be \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\).  \nSince it stays left of all holes of its rows, we have \\(d<\\min_{i\\in[a,b]}p(i)\\).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the column of its left\u2011adjacent cell is \\(p(i)-1\\ge d\\).  \nIf \\(p(i)-1>d\\) the cell \\(\\ell_i\\) lies to the right of the rectangle and is not covered.  \nIf \\(p(i)-1=d\\) then \\(\\ell_i\\) lies on the rightmost column of the rectangle.  \nBecause the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) are distinct, at most one row in \\([a,b]\\) can satisfy \\(p(i)-1=d\\).  \nHence \\(R\\) contains at most one \\(\\ell_i\\). \u220e\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) left\u2011adjacent cells (the unique row with \\(p(i)=1\\) has none).  \nSince each left\u2011type rectangle can cover at most one of them, any tiling needs at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right side  \n\nDefine for each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the **right\u2011adjacent cell**\n\\[\nr_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]  \nThe \\(r_i\\) are the leftmost cells of the right part of each row; they occupy the distinct columns \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThe same argument as in Lemma\u202f1, applied to right\u2011type rectangles (which must satisfy \\(c>\\max_{i\\in[a,b]}p(i)\\)), yields:\n\n\\**Lemma 2.** *A right\u2011type rectangle can contain at most one right\u2011adjacent cell.*\n\nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type rectangles are necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Total lower bound  \n\nAny admissible tiling consists of left\u2011type and right\u2011type rectangles, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left\u2011type tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]  \n\n*Right\u2011type tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]  \n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type and \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type tiles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Verification.*\n\n1. **Each tile is a rectangle.**  \n   Both \\(T^{\\text{L}}_j\\) and \\(T^{\\text{R}}_j\\) consist of a single column and a consecutive block of rows; hence they are axis\u2011parallel rectangles.\n\n2. **Tiles are disjoint.**  \n   Different left\u2011type tiles use different columns, and different right\u2011type tiles also use different columns.  A left\u2011type tile and a right\u2011type tile never share a column, so the families are pairwise disjoint.\n\n3. **Every non\u2011diagonal cell is covered exactly once.**  \n   Let \\((i,j)\\) be a cell with \\(j\\neq i\\).  \n   - If \\(j<i\\) then \\(j\\le i-1\\); the left\u2011type tile \\(T^{\\text{L}}_j\\) contains all rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\), in particular row \\(i\\). Hence \\((i,j)\\in T^{\\text{L}}_j\\).  \n   - If \\(j>i\\) then \\(j\\ge i+1\\); the right\u2011type tile \\(T^{\\text{R}}_j\\) contains rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\), in particular row \\(i\\). Hence \\((i,j)\\in T^{\\text{R}}_j\\).  \n   No other tile contains \\((i,j)\\) because tiles of the same side use distinct columns, and a tile of the opposite side does not contain that column. Thus each non\u2011diagonal cell is covered exactly once.\n\n4. **Exactly one uncovered cell per row and per column.**  \n   The only cells not belonging to any tile are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\); there is precisely one such cell in each row and each column.\n\nHence the construction satisfies all requirements and uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Optimality  \n\nEquation\u202f(1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have reviewed the \u201cSummary\u2019\u2019 and \u201cDetailed Solution\u2019\u2019 sections.  \nAll statements are rigorously proved, 