these two columns, so \\(R\\) contains column \\(p(k)\\).  \nThe row interval of \\(R\\) contains both rows \\(i\\) and \\(k\\); in particular it contains row \\(k\\).  \nHence the cell \\((k,p(k))\\) (the hole in column \\(p(k)\\)) lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the fact that a tile cannot cover a hole. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Corollary 1.**  \nThe set \\(\\{a_i\\mid p(i)<n\\}\\) has cardinality \\(n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\)).  \nSince each rectangle in \\(A\\) can contain at most one such cell, any covering of \\(A\\) by rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nAn analogous statement holds for region \\(B\\): any rectangle contained in \\(B\\) contains at most one cell  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,p(i)-1),\\qquad\\text{for those }i\\text{ with }p(i)>1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe proof is identical to Lemma 1 after reflecting the board horizontally. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Corollary 2.**  \nThe set \\(\\{b_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\) also has size \\(n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\)).  \nThus any covering of \\(B\\) by rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lower bound.**  \nSince the tiles covering the board must partition \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) separately, the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction achieving the bound.**  \nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n* If \\(i>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) (the left side of the hole).  \n* If \\(i<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right side of the hole).\n\nThese tiles are axis‑aligned, pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.  \nThe number of tiles placed is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left‑side tiles (rows }2\\text{ to }n)}\n+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right‑side tiles (rows }1\\text{ to }n-1)}\n=2(n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Optimality.**  \nEquation (1) gives a universal lower bound, while the construction (2) attains this bound.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Evaluation for the given grid.**  \nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid so that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
