dots ,b\\},\\;J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nThe rectangle must avoid all holes, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in I,\\; \\pi(i)\\notin J. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to partition \\(U\\) into the smallest possible number of such\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two uncovered cells  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,\\pi(i+1)),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,\\pi(i)).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth are uncovered because the holes are at \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) and\n\\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nNo rectangle satisfying (1) can contain two distinct cells from the set\n\\(\\mathcal S=\\{A_i,B_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n*Case 1:* \\(R\\) contains \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(i,j\\in I\\) and \\(\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j+1)\\in J\\).  \nSince the rows are consecutive, \\(i+1\\in I\\).  \nBut \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) is the column of the hole in row \\(i+1\\); because\n\\(\\pi(i+1)\\in J\\) and \\(i+1\\in I\\), condition (1) is violated.\nHence this case is impossible.\n\n*Case 2:* \\(R\\) contains \\(B_i\\) and \\(B_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nAnalogously, \\(i+1,j+1\\in I\\) and \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(j)\\in J\\).  \nSince \\(i+1\\in I\\) and \\(\\pi(i)\\) is the hole column of row \\(i\\),\nthe rectangle would contain the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), contradicting (1).\n\n*Case 3:* \\(R\\) contains \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(i\\le j\\).  \nThen \\(i\\in I\\), \\(j+1\\in I\\) (because rows are consecutive) and\n\\(\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j)\\in J\\).  \nSince \\(i+1\\le j+1\\) we have \\(i+1\\in I\\); but \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) is the hole\ncolumn of row \\(i+1\\), so the rectangle would contain the hole\n\\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\), again contradicting (1).\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore a rectangle can\ncontain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the \\(2(n-1)\\) cells of \\(\\mathcal S\\) must all be covered and each\nrectangle can cover at most one of them, any tiling of \\(U\\) needs at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define\n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n      =\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\na horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define\n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n      =\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\na vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*   **Disjointness.**  \n\\(H_i\\) uses only row \\(i\\); \\(V_j\\) uses only column \\(j\\).  \nIf a cell \\((i,j)\\) belonged to both, we would have \\(i=j\\), which is a\nhole \\((i,i)\\) and therefore not part of any rectangle.  Hence the\nrectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*   **Coverage of all uncovered cells.**  \nFor any cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\) (right of the diagonal) we have\n\\(j\\ge i+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).  \nFor any cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) (left of the diagonal) we have\n\\(i\\ge j+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).  \nThus every non‑hole cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles\n\\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\).\n\nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nEquation (3) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles for any\npermutation of holes.  \nEquation (6) exhibits a concrete tiling that attains this bound.  \nConsequently the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Application to the given board\n\nFor the problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\text{ number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
