complement of the holes.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   The universal lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows and columns by \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Reformulation\n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:[n]\\longrightarrow [n]\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nAll other \\(n^{2}-n\\) cells must be covered by the tiles.\n\nA tile is an axis\u2011aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently it is a product  \n\n\\[\nR=I\\times J,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\subseteq[n]\\) and \\(J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\}\\subseteq[n]\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns.  \nThe rectangle must avoid all holes, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in I,\\qquad \\pi(i)\\notin J. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to partition the set of covered cells into the smallest possible number of such rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A universal lower bound\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) consider the \\(2\\times2\\) sub\u2011grid formed by rows \\(i,i+1\\) and columns \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\).  \nIts four cells are  \n\n\\[\n(i,\\pi(i)),\\;(i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\quad\\text{(the holes)},\n\\]\n\\[\nA_i=(i,\\pi(i+1)),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) are ordinary squares that must be covered.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nNo rectangle satisfying (1) can contain both \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) contains \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\).  \nThen \\(i\\in I\\) and \\(i+1\\in I\\) (since the rows of the two cells are \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\)), so \\(I\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{i,i+1\\}\\).  \nSimilarly, the columns of the two cells are \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(i)\\); thus \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\in J\\), and \\(J\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\}\\).  \nConsequently the rectangle \\(R\\) contains the entire \\(2\\times2\\) block, in particular the diagonal cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), which is a hole.  \nThis contradicts condition (1). \u220e\n\n---\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the set \\(\\{A_i,B_i\\}\\) contributes two distinct covered cells, and Lemma\u202f1 shows that a single rectangle can cover **at most one** of them.  \nSince the pairs are disjoint for different \\(i\\), the total number of cells that must be covered in this way is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=2(n-1),\n\\qquad \\mathcal S:=\\{A_i,B_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence any tiling of the board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the holes are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define a horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define a vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n*Verification.*\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nEach \\(H_i\\) occupies only row \\(i\\); each \\(V_j\\) occupies only column \\(j\\).  \nIf a cell belonged to both a horizontal and a vertical rectangle, it would have coordinates \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i=j\\), i.e. a diagonal cell, which is a hole and therefore not part of any rectangle.  \nThus all rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Avoidance of holes.*  \nEvery rectangle \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\); the hole in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(i\\), which is not among them.  \nEvery rectangle \\(V_j\\) uses rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\); the hole in column \\(j\\) is at row \\(j\\), which is not among them.  \nHence condition (1) holds for all rectangles.\n\n*Coverage of all non\u2011hole cells.*  \nTake any cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c>r\\) (to the right of the diagonal).  \nThen \\(c\\in\\{r+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((r,c)\\in H_r\\).  \nIf \\(r>c\\) (below the diagonal), then \\(r\\in\\{c+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((r,c)\\in V_c\\).  \nThus every cell that is not a hole belongs to exactly one rectangle.\n\nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nEquation\u202f(2) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles for any placement of the holes.  \nEquation\u202f(3) exhibits a concrete tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\text{ number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
