"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of holes.**  \n   - Let the unique uncovered square in row\u202f\\(i\\) be at column \\(p(i)\\).  \n   - Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation (\\(n=2025\\)).\n\n2. **Two regions.**  \n   - Define  \n     \\[\n     A=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\},\\qquad \n     B=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\}.\n     \\]\n   - Every covered cell belongs to \\(A\\) (right of its row\u2011hole) or \\(B\\) (left of its row\u2011hole).  \n   - A rectangle cannot contain a hole, therefore each tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).\n\n3. **Critical cells and a lower bound.**  \n   - For each row with \\(p(i)<n\\) consider the cell immediately to the right of the hole:\n     \\[\n     a_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n     \\]\n     The set \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) has size \\(n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\)).  \n   - **Lemma 1.** Any rectangle contained in \\(A\\) can contain **at most one** such cell \\(a_i\\).  \n     *Proof sketch:* If a rectangle contained two distinct cells \\(a_i,a_k\\) with \\(i<k\\) and, say, \\(p(i)<p(k)\\), then the rectangle\u2019s column interval would contain the column \\(p(k)\\). Since its row interval contains row \\(k\\), the hole \\((k,p(k))\\) would lie inside the rectangle \u2013 impossible. \u220e  \n   - Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all of \\(A\\).  \n   - An analogous argument applied to the cells immediately left of each hole,\n     \\[\n     b_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\quad(p(i)>1),\n     \\]\n     shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(B\\).\n\n   - **Lower bound:** every admissible tiling uses at least  \n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n     \\]\n     tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\):\n     * if \\(i>1\\), place a horizontal \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile covering columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\) (the left side of the hole);\n     * if \\(i<n\\), place a horizontal \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile covering columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) (the right side of the hole).\n   - These tiles are axis\u2011aligned, pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.  \n   - The number of tiles placed is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\).\n\n5. **Optimality.**  \n   - The universal lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction achieving it coincide; therefore the minimum number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\n6. **Evaluation for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   \\[\n   2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition into two regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>p(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered unit square lies either to the right of its row\u2011hole (in \\(A\\)) or to the left (in \\(B\\)).  \n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain a hole; consequently a tile is completely contained in \\(A\\) or completely contained in \\(B\\).  \nThus the tiling problem splits into tiling the set \\(A\\) and tiling the set \\(B\\) by axis\u2011aligned rectangles that do not overlap.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Critical cells on the right side  \n\nFor each row with \\(p(i)<n\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nThe cells \\(a_i\\) are precisely the squares immediately to the right of the holes; they lie in \\(A\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the columns \\(p(i)+1\\) are all distinct, and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{a_i\\mid p(i)<n\\}\\,|=n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*Any rectangle contained in \\(A\\) contains at most one cell of the form \\(a_i\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq A\\) contains two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nLet \\(c_i=p(i)+1\\) and \\(c_k=p(k)+1\\) be their column indices.  \nWithout loss of generality suppose \\(p(i)<p(k)\\); then \\(c_i\\le p(k)\\le c_k\\).\n\nSince \\(R\\) contains columns \\(c_i\\) and \\(c_k\\), its column interval contains every column between them, in particular column \\(p(k)\\).  \nIts row interval contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), hence also row \\(k\\).  \nTherefore the cell \\((k,p(k))\\) (the hole in row \\(k\\)) lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the fact that a tile cannot cover a hole. \u220e\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and (2) we obtain:\n\n#### Corollary 1  \nAt least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(A\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Critical cells on the left side  \n\nFor each row with \\(p(i)>1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,p(i)-1),\n\\]\n\nthe squares immediately to the left of the holes; they lie in \\(B\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\), so  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{b_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\,|=n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument as in Lemma\u202f1 (after reflecting the board horizontally) yields:\n\n#### Lemma 2  \n*Any rectangle contained in \\(B\\) contains at most one cell of the form \\(b_i\\).*\n\nConsequently, by (3),\n\n#### Corollary 2  \nAt least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Universal lower bound  \n\nSince the tiles covering the board must partition \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) separately, the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\):\n\n* If \\(i>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) covering the cells  \n  \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) (the left side of the hole).\n* If \\(i<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) covering the cells  \n  \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right side of the hole).\n\nThese tiles are axis\u2011aligned, pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.  \nThe number of tiles placed is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left\u2011side tiles (rows }2,\\dots ,n)}\n+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right\u2011side tiles (rows }1,\\dots ,n-1)}\n=2(n-1). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (4) is achievable.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Optimality and final answer  \n\nEquations\u202f(4) and (5) show that the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).  \nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
